posts.body:
@Meteodros
your second option is already possible (check the forum for tutorials about using damage formual coding) and the number of sample maps already went up and is above a hundred for MV.
As for your first question: deployment to mobile (android/iOS) is now possible with MV and they will certainly check how much that was used before deciding to add more options in a future maker. And don't forget that in the case of handhelds, you usually have to pay a fee to be allowed to program on them. That will limit the use of RM there because a lot of users don't want to spend that money on a non-commercial game.

@Rukiri
there are reasons and advantages to grid-based maps, and some of those advantages are vital for things like deployment to mobile. A lot of those reasons are not obvious to the casual user as they are dependent on other decisions and parts.
A lot of it is because the RMs have always based on an interpreter, not a compiler - both hardware accelleration and C# (if I'm not wrong, not a C# user) are compiler-based and difficult to do on an interpreter basis. And changing from an interpreter engine to a compiler engine would have several disadvantages to the way the community handles the projects (despite writing an compiler being a lot of work more than using an interpreter for the engine)

@bgillisp
: it isn't that 3D wouldn't work - the problem is that adding a new 3D object requires a specific set of skills that are much harder to learn than making a new 2D tile for the map you're currently working on. It isn't by accident that most free 3D games use the same models all again.
And ask one of our users how to make a 2D tile of something new, and ask him how to make a 3D-model of something new. In the first case he can usually try himself in MS Paint if nothing else - I don't think that more than a few of those even know which programs to look for to make a 3d model...

@Andar
: You might want to look at how that program did it then. They didn't use 3d objects at all. Instead, it was tiles or flat images, projected into a first person view. So instead of providing a 3d model, they would instead have to provide what the tile looked like at point blank, 1 step away, 2 steps away, and 3 steps away (and I think the engine stopped at 3 steps for view, not sure anymore).
The art load is a little higher due to the need to provide it from all angles (left/right/top/down) and 4 distances, but other than that, it worked well, and people managed to add their own art to the program. So I think it's because those things exist and have for 20+ years is why people are shocked no one has tried since then to make an engine like it.

@MobileSuitSonic
To get the kid-tab in the generaotr, you need to install or update the core program to 1.3.4 or 1.3.5
But that will only get you one option each for free, because the main content of the child generator parts are currently the season pass DLC4 on steam, those parts are not free

@Kest
there already is a community plugin to change tilesizes, and there are valid reasons to not make the tilesize variable by default
Things like the need to provide other tilesize tilesets if the default gives that option, and that would drive the cost for the stock resources up a lot.

Pixel based event placement and size;
Even more useful along with the suggestion bellow.
Custom object (character) placement via editor;
I think this would be really useful for plugins, like action battle systems or projectiles.
Possibility to move using speed and angle (rotation);
Input confingurations via editor;
Sprite configurations:
Create a sprite editor similar to the animations one;
The character sprites are no longer a reference for the image files, instead they are reference for the sprite object in the database;
If the sprite has an animation, the animation will always be playing on the character (for the current sprite set on the character);
Have a walking sprite object with animation by default, so people that are not familiar with this or dont want to use dont need to bother;
Add the command to change sprite;
This would make really easy to add custom emotions and other stuff, like skill animations, using weapons or tools, idle animation... the list goes on.
Collision system for objects;
At least a square based, bounding boxes.
If added the sprite configuration, this could be set on the sprite object;
Configure dialog and choice windows:
Define width, lines and location;
There's probably more, but I need to go out for lunch (at work :x).
Also, I know these are very unlikely to be, RPG Maker is meant to be easy and friendly for people who are not familiar with game development. We need to keep that in mind.
